---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout
  title="The Gambia: Plastic Loop"
  description="Plastic Loop Gambia establishes a locally rooted system for plastic collection, sorting, processing and manufacturing in the Greater Banjul Area, led by Hustle for Humanity Gambia."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal"><a href={base} style="color:inherit;">&larr; Plastic Circle West Africa</a></p>
      <h1 class="reveal">Plastic Loop, The Gambia</h1>
      <p class="lede reveal">
        A locally rooted circular economy model that converts unmanaged
        plastic waste in the Greater Banjul Area into marketable products,
        jobs and longer-term local productive capacity.
      </p>

      <div class="stat-bar">
        <div class="stat">
          <p class="stat-label">Plastic waste generated</p>
          <p class="stat-value">~23,000 t / year</p>
          <p class="stat-note">about 8.5 kg per person &middot; plastic is ~9% of all municipal solid waste (UNDP, 2023)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <p class="stat-label">Unmanaged plastic</p>
          <p class="stat-value">~75%</p>
          <p class="stat-note">leaks into the environment, rivers and the Atlantic Ocean; could rise 42% by 2033 without intervention (Common Seas, 2024)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <p class="stat-label">Greater Banjul Area</p>
          <p class="stat-value">~1 million people</p>
          <p class="stat-note">the country's administrative, commercial and cultural hub, growing at ~4.2% a year</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Local context</p>
        <h2>Why The Gambia, why now</h2>
        <p>
          The Gambia is Africa's smallest mainland country, ranking 174th out
          of 193 on the Human Development Index, with a national poverty
          rate of around 48% (World Bank, 2022) and roughly 84% of employed
          youth in informal work (GBoS, 2023). Most waste is disposed of at
          informal dumpsites or burned in backyards; uncollected waste
          pollutes the River Gambia and its mangrove forests on its way to
          the Atlantic.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Gambia is also one of the few countries whose climate
          commitments are recognised as aligned with the Paris Agreement's
          1.5&deg;C goal, and a circular economy network has already begun to
          take shape in the Greater Banjul Area, creating momentum for
          sustainable resource management that Plastic Loop builds on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section panel wine" style="margin: 0 clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 2.5rem); width:auto; padding: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem) clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);">
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color: var(--blush);">Lead implementation partner</p>
    <h2>Hustle for Humanity Gambia</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 680px;">
      Hustle for Humanity Gambia (HFH-G), led by <strong>Musa Jallow</strong>,
      is the local implementation lead for Plastic Loop: day-to-day
      coordination, monitoring, community engagement, stakeholder
      mobilisation and practical implementation on the ground in the Greater
      Banjul Area. It works alongside Ayara Foundation Switzerland, which
      leads strategy, fundraising and international partnerships, and
      Aquitas (Switzerland) Ltd., which provides monitoring, evaluation,
      accountability and learning (MEAL) and evidence support.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Approach</p>
        <h2>From collection to local manufacturing</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid reveal">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Recover</h3>
          <p>
            A network of clearly labelled plastic collection points at
            schools, markets and community centres, combined with regular
            community collection drives run with schools, youth groups and
            women's associations, feeds a structured sourcing model.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Prepare</h3>
          <p>
            Plastic is sorted by resin type, colour and quality against a
            resin-based acceptance matrix, cleaned and prepared for
            production, with roughly 60&ndash;75% of collected material
            expected to enter productive use.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Make</h3>
          <p>
            Technology transferred through the ECOLLABO8 partnership &mdash;
            moulded products, sheet press, flow moulding extrusion and
            extrusion press &mdash; converts recovered plastic into
            furniture, outdoor products, surface materials, household items
            and bespoke solutions for schools, hospitality and offices.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Sell &amp; reinvest</h3>
          <p>
            Product development happens in collaboration with local
            furniture makers, construction firms, hospitality operators and
            retailers, who also act as market and distribution channels, with
            surpluses reinvested into jobs and growth.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Operational resilience</p>
        <h2>Built for the Gambian coast</h2>
        <p>
          Gambian businesses continue to experience frequent power outages,
          with many relying on generators to keep operating. Plastic Loop
          integrates a hybrid solar, battery and generator energy system
          calibrated to the Gambian coast's seasonal solar profile, with
          separate dry- and wet-season planning so that production
          continuity does not depend on grid power alone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Partners</p>
        <h2>Delivered together</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid reveal">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Ayara Foundation Switzerland</h3>
          <p>Programme lead: strategy, technical direction, governance, fundraising and international partnerships.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Hustle for Humanity Gambia</h3>
          <p>Local implementation lead: community engagement, day-to-day coordination and stakeholder mobilisation, led by Musa Jallow.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>ECOLLABO8</h3>
          <p>Technology partner: recycling machinery, technical onboarding, production know-how and training.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Aquitas (Switzerland)</h3>
          <p>MEAL and evidence partner: baselines, indicators, data quality and outcome monitoring.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section panel wine" style="margin: 0 clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 2.5rem) clamp(3rem, 8vw, 6rem); width:auto; padding: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem) clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem); text-align:center;">
    <h2>Support Plastic Loop Gambia</h2>
    <p style="max-width:560px; margin: 0 auto 1.5em;">
      Ayara Foundation Switzerland is seeking grant, co-funding and strategic
      partners for Plastic Loop, including full-site and component
      financing.
    </p>
    <a class="pill-btn outline on-dark" href={`${base}contact`}>Get in Touch</a>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .pill-btn.outline.on-dark {
    border-color: var(--blush);
    color: var(--blush-pale);
  }

  .pill-btn.outline.on-dark:hover {
    background: var(--blush-pale);
    color: var(--wine-dark);
  }
</style>
